title: Restoring from the trash
tags: Trash
kind: guide
summary: Put a trashed file back where it was, with its metarecords, from the trash panel, the command line, or an undo.

There are two ways back.

!! Restore the entry

* In the [[trash panel]]: select the entry and press <<key "trash:restore" Enter>>.
* From the command line: `mf trash list` to find the entry's id, then `mf trash restore <id>` (<<cmd "mf trash restore">>).

The file goes back to the path it was trashed from, and its metarecords come
back at their original uuids, with their fields ([[What restoring does]]). A
restore never overwrites anything: if something now occupies the path, move it
aside first. A directory is the exception — restored onto an existing directory,
it is merged into it, as long as no file would be overwritten.

!! Undo the trashing

A trashing is an ordinary revision of the event log, so `mf log undo`, or
`log:undo` in the GUI (<<key "log:undo" Ctrl+Z>>), takes it back — file included.
A redo trashes it again. See [[Trash, undo and redo]].

Restoring the entry works even after the log has been pruned; undo needs the
trashing to still be in the log.
